title A cure that reached one of two branches
prompt The baton -- take the next agent-doable lap, reds first, on the Patchouli seat.
rota lap 4319, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in; it supplied the reading that resolved the red. The threshold page says the concrete fact outranks the plausible story on this row, and here the plausible story was the guard's own comment claiming the cure was complete.
think The cold roster refused first: two orphaned passes from my previous lap held construction/standing-equipment-run.lock.d, launched detached with nohup and outliving the lap that named them. I read them with fleet_call.sh --pattern rather than signalling by name, confirmed both in this tree, and let the live one finish rather than killing work already twenty minutes in.
think It answered guards_red=3. standing_equipment is a self-red over the other two; stash_record unlanded=3 is my own %636, which is Keaton's cut. That left crushed_index red, index_unlisted=1 -- press/date has no row on docs-geode/press/README.md.
think Reading the scan, its two branches build a room's member list differently. The deep branch drops date/, archive/ and yonder/ on the tree's own read-scope law, with a four-line comment seated 20260906 explaining the class. The one-level branch takes cut -d/ -f1 and carries no exclusion at all, so the first path component of press/date/20260907/x.html arrives as the member `date`.
think The earth reading is what made this quick rather than long. The file says twice that it is finished -- its member definition names only README.md as excluded, and its deep-branch comment calls that branch "the one place in this file that disagreed with it". Both sentences were written by a hand that had just fixed one branch, and both are false one screen down. The concrete fact was the scan's output; the plausible story was the file's own prose about itself.
think The exclusion runs before the cut rather than after, and that ordering is the whole precision. The pattern needs a trailing slash, so date/20260907/x.html drops while a tracked file named exactly `date` keeps no slash, stays a member, and still owes its row.
obs Trade-off taken on scope: the Lindy crux in my own lane is the port of mantra/src/main.rye onto weave.rye, which would take %589's disagreements from 2 to 1 and delete roughly two hundred lines of inlined model and LCS that mantra/src/diff.rye already carries bounded. I surveyed it whole and left it, because reds-first outranks the queue and a guard answering red stops the line. The survey stands in this log so the next lap opens on it rather than rediscovering it.
obs Against myself: the file-named-`date` control case was first planted into the pen that already holds a DIRECTORY of that name. A path cannot be both, the write failed silently under its own redirect, and the case read 0 -- looking exactly like a refutation of the claim it was written to prove. It has its own pen now, and the near-miss is recorded on the shelf row.
obs Two of the six new control cases pass on the elder scan as well, which would have left them proving nothing. Rather than delete them I built the spelling they actually guard -- a variant that cuts first and excludes the bare word after -- and both bite it. Four cases bite the missing exclusion; two bite the wrong ordering; every one was run against a wrong scan and refused before this shipped.
obs The pin was deadlocked for the fourth time in one day: pin_headroom=24, 13 rows, all OPEN, pin_foldable_rows=0, rows_that_fit=0. I took COPAL's precedent and bore the row on its own single-row shelf, recited it, and the row is CLOSED so shelf_open_rows does not move. One thing the precedent does not solve and I am surfacing: a shelf-born row still has to edit the deadlocked pin's own headline, since reds_ledger_monotone reads the total, span and remainder off it. The three numbers are same-width here so the bytes did not move, and that is luck rather than design.
